OS Coupling Assessment Knowledge Base

KB ID: OS-COUPLING-001
Version: 1.0
Domain: Application Architecture
Scope: Operating System Coupling

1. Purpose

Identify application dependencies on operating-system-specific behavior, configuration, APIs, commands, services, libraries, filesystem semantics, permissions, kernel behavior, devices, CPU architecture, or OS/runtime versions.

The key question is:

Could an OS upgrade, OS migration, kernel change, host-environment change, or CPU architecture change break the application?

The assessment must identify:

What the application depends on

Where the dependency exists

Which repository/component contains the dependency

Who owns the dependency

Whether the dependency is direct or indirect

Whether it is host-level or container-internal

Whether an OS change can break the application

Severity and confidence

Recommended remediation

2. Application Types in Scope

The assessment must support:

Bare-metal applications

VM-based applications

Containerized applications

Kubernetes applications

ECS/container-platform applications

Monolithic applications

Microservices

Serverless applications

Batch applications

Data-processing applications

Legacy applications

Desktop applications

Vendor-packaged applications

Hybrid applications

Applications spanning multiple deployment models

Do not assume that an application has only one repository or one execution model.

3. Multi-Repository Assessment

An application may contain multiple repositories.

Example:

Payment Application
├── payment-api
├── payment-batch
├── payment-engine
├── payment-container
├── payment-deployment
└── payment-infrastructure

Assess the complete application landscape.

Evidence may be distributed across repositories.

Example:

Repository A:

Application code:
open("/etc/payment/config")

Repository B:

hostPath:
  path: /etc/payment

The combination establishes:

Application
    ↓
Container
    ↓
Host filesystem
    ↓
Host OS

This is stronger evidence of OS coupling than either repository considered independently.

