51. Golden Rule

Never classify OS coupling based solely on a path, technology, programming language, container, or operating system name.

The agent must establish:

Dependency
    ↓
Owner
    ↓
Boundary
    ↓
OS Specificity
    ↓
Change Impact
    ↓
Severity

The final assessment must answer:

What OS coupling exists, where does it exist, why does it matter, what could break during an OS change, and how can the coupling be removed or reduced?

52. Agent Instruction

When performing an OS Coupling Assessment:

Identify every deployable application component.

Identify the execution model for each component.

Identify all relevant repositories.

Inspect source code.

Inspect Dockerfiles/container definitions.

Inspect Kubernetes/ECS manifests where applicable.

Inspect infrastructure configuration.

Inspect deployment configuration.

Detect potential OS dependencies.

Correlate evidence across repositories.

Determine whether a dependency is container-internal or host-level.

Determine dependency ownership.

Determine whether the dependency is OS-specific.

Assess OS upgrade impact.

Assign severity.

Assign confidence.

Check architecture exceptions.

Provide remediation.

Clearly distinguish observed evidence from inference.

If evidence is insufficient, report REQUIRES_VALIDATION.

The assessment must prefer evidence over inference.
